[00:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This strange-looking product is a toroidal propeller, and it could fundamentally change our understanding of propulsion systems. This unconventional geometry has attracted a lot of attention over the past few years, with'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'claims of lower noise levels, weaker tip vortices, and even increased efficiency. And if true, it could completely change the future of aviation, opening the door to entirely new'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ways of flying. But there is one problem. No one seems to be able to agree on how good they really are or even what the optimal toroidal propeller should look like. There are a multitude of concepts, patents, and'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prototypes, but there is remarkably little agreement on which geometry works best and why. So in this video I\'m going to try to figure that out. If you\'ve seen my previous videos, you'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'already know that I aim to create the ultimate 3D printed ultra-efficient flying wing. And if toroidal propellers really deliver what they promise, they could be the perfect addition. So, I will reverse'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'engineer a conventional propeller , create a fully parametric toroidal model from scratch, optimize hundreds of geometries using CFD and artificial intelligence, and then manufacture and test the best ones'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on my bench and in real flights. And by the end of this video, we should finally have an answer to one very simple question. Can a properly optimized toroidal propeller actually'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'outperform a conventional one? It\'s time to separate the hype from the engineering, because the final result may really surprise you. Although toroidal'

[01:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'propellers look futuristic, the idea itself is over 130 years old. In 1892, British inventor Charles Myers patented a marine propeller with closed blades attached to a'

[01:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hub at both ends, which is very similar in concept to what we now call a toroidal propeller. But this idea never managed to compete with conventional propellers and was forgotten for over a century. That'

[02:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'changed in 2017 when researchers at MIT Lincoln Laboratory revisited this concept for small drones, demonstrating comparable thrust at similar power, but with a much quieter'

[02:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'acoustic signature. And theoretically, the reason for this is surprisingly simple. In a conventional propeller, the pressure difference across the blade forces air to flow over the free tip, creating a strong tail vortex. This is'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'essentially the same mechanism that creates the trailing vortices on an airplane wing, only here the blade is constantly rotating, generating a spiral vortex that drags behind the propeller. By looping the tip'

[02:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', a toroidal propeller changes the way these vortices form and dissipate, potentially reducing both aerodynamic losses and noise. Well, at least that\'s what the theory says, but I want'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to see it for myself . So, my goal is simple. Design a toroidal propeller from scratch and try to surpass a conventional one in efficiency. And not just any'

[03:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ordinary propeller. As a reference, I will use the Aeronaut Power Prop 9x7. If you remember my previous video, this propeller was the efficiency winner for a specific'

[03:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'endurance mission on my NX 2 aircraft. So, these will be the exact design requirements for a toroidal propeller. To create about 0.5 newtons of thrust at 13 m/s while consuming'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as little energy as possible. So first we need to know the exact characteristics of a commercial propeller, establishing a clear benchmark for comparing both designs under the'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'same conditions. In previous projects, I have used photogrammetry to reverse engineer larger objects, but for such a small propeller, I need a much higher'

[03:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'resolution, preferably less than 0.1 mm. So this time I will use a high-resolution 3D scanner . The process is quite simple. I slowly'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'move the scanner around the propeller to capture the surface from all possible angles. Then I flip it over and repeat the scan to cover the opposite side. Then, on the computer, both scans are aligned and'

[04:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'combined. Residual gaps and noise are cleaned up, and the final mesh is processed into a full 3D model. This gives me a very accurate digital replica of the Aeronaut propeller that I can'

[04:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use as a reference to reproduce the clean geometry in my CAD software. You may be wondering why I put so much effort into a conventional propeller before moving on to a toroidal one, and there'

[04:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are two main reasons for this. First, this digital model allows me to study what exactly makes a modern propeller work. For example: airfoil profiles, twist angle, chord distribution, and'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how all of these elements vary along the blade. But more importantly, with the exact geometry, I can do this. Full-fledged transient CFD modeling of a'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'real propeller. Setting up this simulation was a challenge. Simulating just 0.1 second of real propeller motion, or about seven revolutions, took my computer'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'over two full days. But all these calculations give us a highly accurate baseline of how a typical propeller actually behaves. And how effective is it? To measure this, we will'

[05:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use the efficiency of the motion, which compares the useful power (thrust multiplied by flight speed) with the mechanical power required to rotate the propeller ( torque and rotational speed).'

[05:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'At 13 m/s and 4300 rpm, the Aeronaut propeller achieves approximately 73% efficiency—an extremely high figure for such a small propeller. And at this point the challenge is'

[05:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'officially accepted. Design a toroidal propeller that can exceed 73% efficiency. But before we continue, let me take a short pause. You probably already know that I\'ve'

[06:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'been using Onshape for quite some time . So today I want to show you something that they just released that I honestly think will change the way we approach CAD automation. This is called FeatureScript NCP, and it'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'essentially allows you to connect Onshape directly to an AI agent. In my case, I use Codex, and it takes a very smart approach: from text to code to model. You simply describe the'

[06:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'special features you need in plain language, and then the AI generates a script, inserts it into Onshape, runs it, finds errors, and continues to improve the code until it works. And let me tell you, it\'s'

[06:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'truly impressive. For someone who is used to spending days creating a complex parametric model , being able to create something like this in minutes is a huge shift. So, if you\'re working on your own engineering'

[06:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'projects and want to try out this AI-powered CAD workflow, be sure to check out Onshape. And if you\'re an engineer, you can also get up to 6 months of the pro version for free using the link in the description.'

[07:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Unfortunately, I found out about FeatureScript NCP a little late. Therefore, I initially created my toroidal parametric model the hard way. The overall toroidal'

[07:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shape alone is controlled by 17 parameters. I can independently vary the width of the leading and trailing edge loops, which essentially determines how open or compact'

[07:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'each side of the toroidal shape becomes. Two more parameters determine where along each loop the maximum width occurs, significantly changing the overall shape and curvature of the toroidal geometry. And if we now look at the'

[07:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'propeller from the side, two more parameters control the vertical position of the leading and trailing edge hinges , changing the relative height and therefore the overall three-dimensional shape of the structure. Next comes the chord distribution, which'

[08:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is controlled by five independent parameters. Starting from the leading edge loop, I define the chord at three spans: one near the root at about 15%, another at 65%, and a final'

[08:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'control point near the tip at 90%. Here, both loops have the same chord value to simplify and smooth out this complex transition. The trailing edge loop follows the same'

[08:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'approach with its own breakpoints at 15% and 65% of the swing. Finally, we move on to one of the key aerodynamic controls: the six parameters that'

[08:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'determine the local angle of the profile. These stations are located at exactly the same control points as the chord parameters: 15%, 65%, and 90% of the span. But in this case, I'

[08:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'leave the tip angle independent because, as we\'ll see later, this zone is critical to the behavior of the vortex at the tip, and I want to have as much control over it as possible . And at this point we'

[09:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'finally have a fully parametric toroidal propeller. I like to think that somewhere among these 17 variables, there is one particular combination , one geometry, that is simply better than all the others. So now we'

[09:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just need to find it. But with 17 variables, even a broad search quickly becomes impossible. Checking just 10 values ​​for each parameter would mean about 100 quadrillion different'

[09:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'combinations. So " brute force" is obviously not an option. The first thing I need is a much faster way to evaluate each geometry, and that\'s where MRF, the Multiple'

[09:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Reference Frame Method, comes in. I don\'t want to go into technical details, but instead of physically rotating a propeller through a mesh, MRF captures the geometry and calculates the flow inside a rotating frame of reference. This loses the'

[09:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'detailed non-stationary trace, but it is much faster, taking only about 40 minutes per geometry. My time frame for finding the optimal propeller was only about 2 months, so this approach fundamentally'

[10:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'changes everything. But even then, the space of design solutions remains too large for a blind search. And this is where artificial intelligence comes into play . The idea is simple . Instead of testing'

[10:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'random geometries, I create a carefully distributed set of about 500 geometries that explores as much of the 17- dimensional design space as possible. For each geometry, I'

[10:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'automatically generate a CAD model, run a CFD simulation at 2 rpm, and obtain thrust, torque, and efficiency values. But this initial data is just a starting point, because with enough'

[10:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'CFD results, we can start looking for hidden patterns in them . And if there\'s anything designed to detect patterns in complex data, it\'s a neural network. Based on the CFD results,'

[10:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the network begins to study how these 17 parameters affect the final performance of the propeller. And after training, it can evaluate thousands of new designs almost instantly, instead of'

[11:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'spending hours on each CFD simulation. Essentially, the AI ​​identifies the most promising areas, after which I generate a new batch of propellers, simulate them in CFD, add the results back to the database, and'

[11:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'retrain the network. Then the cycle begins again. With each iteration, the neural network narrows the search, discarding ineffective areas and delving into the most'

[11:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'promising ones. And while this is happening, the propeller itself seems to be evolving. The efficiency continues to increase, and after weeks of calculations and hundreds of simulations, the improvements begin to fade. The search for the optimal'

[11:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'propeller is finally coming to an end, and among the roughly one hundred quadrillion possible combinations, one geometry stands out from the rest. This is G-1401, and it comes out on top,'

[11:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'achieving 62.9% efficiency. Unfortunately , we are still significantly inferior to a conventional propeller , but I\'m not giving up yet, because there is one fundamental part of the design'

[12:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that I haven\'t even touched on. Aerodynamic profile. So far I have used the Selyg SD7003—a well-known profile for low Reynolds numbers, but there is no reason to believe that'

[12:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it is optimal for such an unusual propeller. The full workflow is available on my Patreon, and I don\'t want to bore you with all the details, but in short : I capture the overall'

[12:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'winning geometry and optimize the profile itself using 18 more variables and a new AI-powered search. And then, as before, the search begins to narrow until only one geometry remains.'

[12:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'After two months of living purely in the digital world, I was more than ready to'

[13:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'start turning designs into real details. But turning this geometry into a real working 10- inch propeller is a whole other challenge. The requirements are harsh. I need very high'

[13:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dimensional accuracy, an extremely smooth surface, and a material strong enough to withstand thousands of revolutions per minute. And that\'s actually one of the main reasons why I wanted to work with the Formlabs Form 4L in the first place.'

[13:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There is no other printer that combines the level of precision I need with engineered materials like the Tough 2000, making it possible to produce prototypes like this right at home. So I'

[13:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'took the winning geometry with an optimized profile, made small modifications around the hub to reinforce the area that would bear the main mechanical load, and finally it was time to print the first'

[13:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prototype. The workflow itself is surprisingly simple. I import the final geometry into PreForm, choose the orientation, create supports, and send everything directly to Form 4L. After printing,'

[14:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it undergoes a'

[14:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'washing step to remove uncured resin, followed by polymerization to achieve the final mechanical properties. Then I remove the supports and'

[14:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do a light sanding and polishing to remove any traces of them . But even when the final result looks incredible, one serious problem becomes apparent. The optimizer did its job a little'

[14:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'too well. In the pursuit of aerodynamic efficiency, some areas have become too thin, which is unacceptable from a structural point of view. So, unfortunately, this meant'

[14:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the need for another design iteration. This time, it\'s about finding the right balance between aerodynamic efficiency, manufacturability, and structural integrity. I printed a few more'

[15:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'variations, trying to find a balance without losing too much in aerodynamic performance. And before approving the final version, I did a quick structural analysis to'

[15:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make sure it would withstand the high-rpm loads. And after months of design, simulations, optimization, and several printing stages, here is the result. A toroidal propeller that will'

[15:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'finally pass the real test. I started by testing both propellers,'

[15:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'regular and toroidal, on my stand under the same conditions. If you look at thrust per watt, the toroidal propeller is already slightly inferior to the conventional design. But then'

[15:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something interesting happened. I also recorded the acoustic spectrum exactly 1 meter from both propellers using a professionally calibrated microphone. The overall noise level of the'

[16:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'toroidal design has not become significantly lower, but the spectrum is clearly different. A conventional propeller has stronger high-frequency tones and harmonics, which creates the familiar high-frequency hum, while a'

[16:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'toroidal one shifts the energy to lower frequencies, giving a deeper and softer sound. And subjectively the difference is simply huge. Okay, the static'

[16:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'efficiency is clearly worse, but the acoustic picture is definitely better, and there is still a chance to outperform a regular propeller in real flight. So I headed out to the summer field to get the definitive answer. I won\'t'

[16:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'go into all the details, but I repeated the same autonomous mission as in the previous test. If you remember my'

[17:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'previous video, this is the energy consumption curve for the Aeronaut 9 by 7. When we overlay the results of the toroidal propeller, the curve clearly rises upwards, with a minimum of about 0.37 watt-hours'

[17:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'per kilometer, or about 14% more energy consumption. So the answer is no. In the end, the toroidal propeller did not defeat the'

[17:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'conventional one. And after months of work, it\'s of course a little disappointing. But this is what real engineering looks like. Sometimes the most valuable result is not proving that an idea works, but having a'

[17:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'firm understanding of why it does n\'t work. I didn\'t really want to spoil the result prematurely, but the full unsteady CFD simulation had already given us a warning. When'

[17:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all real structural trade-offs were taken into account, the final toroidal design achieved only about 61% efficiency, compared to about 73% for a conventional propeller . Even if it\'s'

[18:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'significantly less, achieving about 60% propulsion efficiency with such a small, home-made propeller with a low Reynolds number is still a pretty impressive result. So, after all of this,'

[18:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what is my honest opinion on toroidal propellers? I find them fascinating, and their acoustic behavior alone makes them worth exploring. In fact, I\'ve already started testing noise-reducing designs like'

[18:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this three-bladed propeller, but I don\'t expect them to replace conventional propellers in general aviation anytime soon. And the reason is clear: looping the blade can weaken the vortex system at the tip and'

[18:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reduce inductive losses, but it also increases the wetted surface, which means more profile drag, surface friction, and torque. So, the toroidal concept does not eliminate aerodynamic losses'

[18:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', it simply replaces one set of losses with another. If you like what I do, please consider joining my Patreon. There you will find the complete workflow, including the geometry, data, files, and tools'

[19:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I used throughout this project. And as always, stay curious, stay creative, and keep building the future. See you in the next video.'
